N2  - Summary This chapter addresses some of the issues of wireless sensors networks when they are used in control system design. It also describes the motivations for using wireless control networks and current wired control theory as it pertains to launch-vehicle control. Using wireless sensors and actuators in a feedback control system can eliminate the extra weight and cost required by a wired system. The chapter gives an overview of deterministic system dynamics and classical control design. A typical deterministic dynamic system is represented by an ordinary differential equation and the order of the differential equation represents the number of variables that vary with time. The chapter also presents a discussion on the effect of constant delays on the closed-loop control system and provides an analysis of the effect of delays on the stability and transient response of first and second-order systems.
